    21	thread_11_summary:Thread 11 = OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS.md §P11 — minimum number of straight lines required to cover all primes <= N under a chosen 2D embedding. Trivially 1 line on Z (primes collinear). Non-trivial in 2D embeddings: (a) Ulam spiral (1963) — diagonal lines correspond to Hardy-Littlewood quadratic-prime sequences f(n) = a*n^2 + b*n + c, dominated by Euler's n^2 + n + 41 and similar; (b) residue-class grid (n_x = n mod p, n_y = n div p), where each row is a prime residue class; (c) polynomial-image grids n -> (P(n), Q(n)) for P, Q polynomial. Quantity: minimum line cover number L(N) of the primes <= N in the chosen embedding. Cross-domain ingredient: Szemeredi-Trotter incidence theorem (1983), Erdos extremal incidence problems, Hardy-Littlewood prime k-tuple conjecture for quadratics. Question: does L(N) admit a polylog or sub-linear computable invariant? Lines of primes correspond to k-tuple-prime polynomial sequences; if covering achieves L(N) ~ pi(N)^alpha for some named alpha < 1, the invariant has algorithmic content for prime-density estimation.
